        "problem": "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 4x=6-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n| $x=$ {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n\n| |  | {{field-2}} | $-2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-4$ | {{field-3}} |  | |  |  |  | |  | $-1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $6$ |  | | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $-2$ |  |  |  |\n|  | $-4$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $6$ |  |  |  |  |",
        "worked_solution": "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 4x+y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
